===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|schadowen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|sċeadwian}}, from {{m|ang|sċeadu|t=shadow; shade}} + {{m|ang|-ian|pos=suffix forming verbs}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[shade]], [[cloud]]{{,}} or [[darken]].
#: {{ux|en|The artist chose to '''shadow''' this corner of the painting.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To block light or radio transmission from.
#: {{ux|en|Looks like that cloud's going to '''shadow''' us.}}
# {{lb|en|particularly|espionage}} To secretly or discreetly track or follow another, to keep under [[surveillance]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2022 November 30|author=Paul Bigland|title=Destination Oban: a Sunday in Scotland|journal=RAIL|issue=971|page=75|text=Soon after departure, we cross the invisible border into Scotland to enjoy more stunning coastal scenery, before the line finally swings inland at Burnmouth to traverse pine-clad valleys, '''shadowed''' by the A1 trunk road until we rejoin the coast at Cove, east of Dunbar.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[represent]] faintly and imperfectly.
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|chapter=36|page=182|passage=Ah, ye admonitions and warnings! why stay ye not when ye come? But rather are ye predictions than warnings, ye '''shadows'''!}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[hide]]; to [[conceal]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} To accompany (a [[professional]]) during the working day, so as to learn about an [[occupation]] one intends to take up.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Alternative Education Models{{...|–Interim Findings from the Replication of Career Intern Program}}|year=1980|chapter=Study of the Career Intern Program|publisher=U.S. Department of Labor{{...|, Employment and Training Administration, Office of Youth Programs}}|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=KGhNqYDt2D4C&dq=intern%20%22shadowed%22&hl=es&pg=PA20#v=onepage&q&f=false|page=20|passage=In most cases, interns have mainly observed, or “'''shadowed''',” their Hands-On hosts, but some interns have been given real tasks to perform, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-av|en|date=February 18, 2022|writer=w:Dan Erickson|title={{w|Severance (TV series)|Severance}}|episode=w:Good News About Hell|season=1|number=1|role=w:Harmony Cobel|actor=w:Patricia Arquette|passage=I know you haven’t run a training before, but Irving will be there to '''shadow'''. Just stick to the flowchart and escalate properly depending on dialectics. You’ll be fine.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|programming}} To make (an [[identifier]], usually a [[variable]]) inaccessible by declaring another of the same name within the [[scope]] of the first.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Mark Summerfield|title=Programming in Go|year=2012|publisher=Addison-Wesley|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=HW-5SZ1HKusC&pg=PA189&dq=%22shadow%22+scope&hl=&cd=10&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22shadow%22%20scope&f=false|isbn=978-0-13-276409-4|page=189|passage=In this snippet, inside the <code>for</code> loop the <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> variables '''shadow''' variables from the outer scope, and while legal, this is almost certainly a programming error.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|computing}} To apply the [[shadowing]] process to (the contents of [[ROM]]).

