[[File:Shield MET DP340275.jpg|thumb|Shield, handheld defensive armour]]

# Anything that [[protect]]s or [[defend]]s; [[defense]]; [[shelter]]; [[protection]].
## {{senseid|en|Q131559}}A broad piece of defensive [[armor]], held in [[hand]], formerly in general use in [[war]], for the [[protection]] of the [[body]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1599|passage=Knock go and come; God's vassals drop and die; And sword and '''shield''', In bloody field, Doth win immortal fame.|author=w:William Shakespeare|title={{w|Henry V (play)|Henry V}}|section=act III, scene II|line=8}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1786|passage=The '''shields''' used by our Norman ancestors were the triangular or heater shield, the target or buckler, the roundel or rondache, and the pavais, pavache, or tallevas.|author=w:Francis Grose|title=A Treatise on Ancient Armour and Weapons|page=22}}
##* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=8|passage=My client welcomed the judge […] and they disappeared together into the Ethiopian card-room, which was filled with the assegais and exclamation point '''shields''' Mr. Cooke had had made at the sawmill at Beaverton.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1961|author={{w|Norma Lorre Goodrich}}|title=The Medieval Myths|publisher=The New American Library|chapter=Beowulf|location=New York|page=44|passage=Beowulf, behind his '''shield''', thrust forth only his right arm.}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}} One who protects or defends.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1611|passage=Fear not, Abram: I am [[thy]] [[shield]], and thy [[exceeding]] great reward.|title=The Holy Bible|edition=King James Version|section={{w|Book of Genesis|Genesis}} 15:1}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1592|passage=Go muster men. My counsel is my '''shield'''; We must be brief when traitors brave the field.|author=w:William Shakespeare|title={{w|Richard III (play)|Richard III}}|section=act 4, scene 3|line=56}}
## {{lb|en|ornithology}} A [[featherless]], flat, fleshy [[protuberance]] on the head; a [[frontal shield]].
## {{lb|en|ornithology}} A flattish [[patch]] of [[iridescent]] feathers.
## {{lb|en|entomology}} A heavily hardened, [[protective]] plate on an insect's [[exoskeleton]] that covers part of its body. 
## {{lb|en|lichenology}} In [[lichen]]s, a hardened cup or disk surrounded by a rim and containing the [[fructification]], or [[asci]].
## {{lb|en|mining|tunnelling}} A [[framework]] used to protect workmen in making an [[adit]] under ground, and capable of being pushed along as [[excavation]] progresses.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Andrew Martin|title=Underground Overground: A passenger's history of the Tube|publisher=Profile Books, {{ISBN|978-1846684777}}|page=88|text=The earth was excavated from the sunken cylinder; the '''shield''' was inserted into it, and the tunnelling began, the target being [[Wapping]], on the opposite bank. The '''shield''' was an iron honeycomb containing thirty-six cells within which men dug the wall of mud before them.}}
## {{lb|en|science fiction}} A field of energy that protects or defends.
##* {{quote-video game|en|developer=w:BioWare|title={{w|Mass Effect (video game)|Mass Effect}}|location=Redwood City|publisher=Electronic Arts|year=2008|system=PC|scene=Kinetic Barriers ("Shields") Codex entry|isbn=9780784546642|oclc=246633669|passage=Kinetic barriers, colloquially called "'''shields'''", provide protection against most mass accelerator weapons. Whether on a starship or a soldier's suit of armor, the basic principle remains the same.<br>Kinetic barriers are repulsive mass effect fields projected from tiny emitters. These '''shields''' safely deflect small objects traveling at rapid velocities. This affords protection from bullets and other dangerous projectiles, but still allows the user to sit down without knocking away their chair.}}
# A [[shape]] like that of a shield; usually, an inverted triangle with sides that curve inward to form a pointed bottom, commonly used for [[police]] identifications and company [[logo]]s.
## {{senseid|en|heraldry}} {{lb|en|heraldry}} The [[escutcheon#English:_heraldry|escutcheon]] on which are placed the [[bearing#English:_heraldry|bearing]]s in [[coats of arms]].
##: {{mer|en|field<id:heraldry: background of the shield>|charge<id:heraldry>|emblem<id:heraldry>}}
##* {{quote-book|en|date=8 October 2012|author=Daniel W. Patterson|publisher=UNC Press Books|isbn=9780807837535|title=The True Image: Gravestone Art and the Culture of Scotch Irish Settlers in the Pennsylvania and Carolina Backcountry|url=https://books.google.co.nz/books?id=s7dGj3J_1cgC|page=141|text=The second and third quarters of the '''shield''' are indecipherable on the stone but clearer in two other representations of the arms, a painted wooden funeral hatchment for Mary Davie{{...}}}}
## {{label|en|Scotland|euphemism|obsolete}} A [[toilet seat]].
## A [[spot]] resembling, or having the [[form]] of a shield.
##* {{quote-book|en|passage=Bespotted as with '''shields''' of red and black.|author=w:Edmund Spenser|title=w:The Faerie Queene|year=1590}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[coin]], the old French [[crown]], or [[écu]], having on one side the figure of a shield.
## {{lb|en|transport}} A sign or symbol, usually containing numbers and sometimes letters, identifying a highway route.
## {{lb|en|law enforcement|colloquial}} A [[police]] [[badge]].
##* {{quote-av|en|date=1995-10-18|author=Morgan Gendal;Barry M. Schkolmick;Michael S. Chernuchin|title=w:Law & Order|episode=Savages|season=6|number=3|time=14:22|actor=Marianne Hagan;Benjamin Bratt|role=Marcie Donner;Detective Rey Curtis|text=M: You get married, that's the last I hear from you. / R: Three kids and a '''shield''', Marcie, I've been busy.}}
