===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|sighen||to sigh}}, {{lg|back-formation}} from {{m|enm|sighte}}, past tense form of {{m|enm|siken}}, from {{der|en|ang|sīcan}}, from {{der|en|gmw-pro|*sīkan}}, perhaps ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*seykʷ-|t=to pour out}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[inhale]] a larger [[quantity]] of [[air]] than usual, and [[immediately]] [[expel]] it; to make a [[deep]] [[single]] [[audible]] [[respiration]], especially as the [[result]] or [[involuntary]] [[expression]] of [[fatigue]], [[exhaustion]], [[grief]], [[sorrow]], [[frustration]], or the [[like]].
#: {{syn|en|suspire|sithe|q1=obsolete}}
#: {{ux|en|When she saw it wasn't damaged, she '''sighed''' with relief.}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''sighed'''. It was going to be a long night.}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''sighed''' over the lost opportunity.}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=5|passage=A waiter brought his aperitif, which was a small scotch and soda, and as he sipped it gratefully he '''sighed'''.<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;‘Civilized,’ he said to Mr. Campion. ‘Humanizing.’ {{...}} ‘Cigars and summer days and women in big hats with swansdown face-powder, that's what it reminds me of.’}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[lament]]; to [[grieve]].
#: {{syn|en|sithe|yearn|q1=obsolete|Thesaurus:be sad}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Mark|8|12|passage=He '''sighed''' deeply in his spirit.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[utter]] sighs over; to lament or [[mourn]] over.
#: {{syn|en|grieve|lament|sithe|q3=obsolete|Thesaurus:lament}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1695|author=w:Matthew Prior|title=An ode presented to the king, on His Majesty's arrival in Holland, after the Queen's death
|passage=Ages to come, and men unborn, / Shall bless her name, and '''sigh''' her fate.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1902|author=John Buchan|title=The Outgoing of the Tide
|passage=The lassie had grace given her to refuse, but with a woeful heart, and Heriotside rode off in black discontent, leaving poor Ailie to '''sigh''' her love. He came back the next day and the next, but aye he got the same answer.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1966|author=H. V. Livermore|title=A New History of Portugal|page=191
|passage=In Lisbon, Queen Luisa, who had '''sighed''' so long for a French marriage, declared that an angel from heaven could not have brought better news.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To make a [[sound]] like sighing.
#: {{syn|en|sithe|q1=obsolete}}
#: {{ux|en|The wind '''sigh'''ed in the trees.}}
#* {{RQ:Wordsworth Coleridge Lyrical Ballads|poem=The Rime of the Ancyent Marinere|passage=And the coming wind did roar more loud, / And the sails did '''sigh''' like sedge.}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Poems 1833|poem=The Death of the Old Year|passage=The winter winds are wearily '''sighing'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[exhale]] (the [[breath]]) in [[#Noun|sigh]]s.
#: {{syn|en|sithe|q1=obsolete}}
#: {{ux|en|She '''sighed''' a sigh that was nearly a groan.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''sigh''' a note and sing a note}}
#: {{ux|en|She '''sigh'''ed her relief when she found her missing toddler.}} 
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Coriolanus|IV|5|passage=Never man '''sighed''' truer breath.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[express]] by sighs; to utter in or with sighs.
#: {{syn|en|sithe|q1=obsolete}}
#: {{ux|en|"I guess I have no choice," she '''sighed'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|She '''sighed''' her frustrations.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Coriolanus|I|1|passage=They {{...}} '''sighed''' forth proverbs.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1763|translator=w:John Hoole|title=Jerusalem Delivered|author=w:Torquato Tasso
|passage=The gentle swain {{...}} '''sighs''' back her grief.}}

