===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|:ubor|fr:silhouette|tree=1}}
The {{lg|noun}} is a {{ubor|en|fr|silhouette|nocap=1}}, from the surname of {{w|Étienne de Silhouette}} (1709–1767), France’s {{w|Controller-General of Finances}}. It has been suggested that the word refers to one or more of the following:<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=2726284555|date=September 2025|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref>

* De Silhouette’s [[penny-pinching]] policies which led to the French term ''à la Silhouette'', applied to things perceived as austere or cheap, akin to an outline filled only with black.
* The fact that de Silhouette only held the office of Controller-General of Finances for about eight months from 4 March to 21 November 1759.
* De Silhouette’s outline portraits which he made to decorate his château at [[Bry-sur-Marne]], [[Val-de-Marne]].

De Silhouette’s surname is a [[gallicize]]d form of {{der|en|eu|Ziloeta}} or {{m|eu|Zilhoeta}} (modern {{cog|eu|Zulueta}}), from the local {{lg|plural}} {{lg|stem}} (see {{m|eu|-ak}}) of {{m|eu|zulo|t=hole, pit; den, hiding place}}, from {{der|en|euq-pro|*zuLo|t=hole}}.

The {{lg|verb}} is derived from the noun.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=9199741476|date=March 2026|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref>

