# {{senseid|en|artistic depiction}}{{lb|en|artistic work}} An [[artistic#Adjective|artistic]] [[depiction]], often of a [[person#Noun|person]]'s [[profile#Noun|profile]], in the [[form#Noun|form]] of an [[outline#Noun|outline]] without any other [[visual#Adjective|visual]] [[detail#Noun|details]], [[similar#Adjective|similar]] to a [[shadow#Noun|shadow]], made by [[cut#Verb|cutting]] a [[piece#Noun|piece]] of [[black#Adjective|black]] [[paper#Noun|paper]], [[fill#Verb|filling]] in an outline with a [[solid#Adjective|solid]] [[color#Noun|colour]] (usually black), etc. {{defdate|late 18th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Lovel|chapter=In which Miss Prior is Kept at the Door|page=60|passage=She had a feeble pencil drawing of the vicarage in Dorsetshire, and '''''silhouettes''''' of her father and mother, which were hung up in the lodgings wherever she went.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=4 April 1907|title=Colonial Relics|editor=w:William Hayes Ward|journal=[[w:The Independent (New York City)|The Independent]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=The Independent,{{nb...|130 Fulton Street, New York}}|volume=LXII|issue=3044|page=812|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/independent62newy/page/812/mode/1up|column=2|oclc=4927591|passage=[A] portion of the {{w|John Boyd Thacher|John Boyd Thatcher}}{{sic}} collection of commissions, documents, autograph letters and portraits; a case containing '''silhouets''' of famous Colonial personages, a '''silhouet''' ring, some smoked '''silhouets''', {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|[[by extension]]}}
## {{senseid|en|dark outline}} A [[dark#Adjective|dark]] outline of someone or something [[see#Verb|seen]] against a [[bright#Adjective|brighter]] [[background#Noun|background]].
##: {{ux|en|I could see a '''silhouette''' of a figure looking out from the window, but I couldn’t tell if it was a man or a woman.}}
##* {{RQ:Thackeray Irish Sketch Book|chapter=Belfast to the Causeway|page=60|passage=All we could see were ghostly-looking '''silhouettes''' of ships gliding here and there through the clouds; and I am sure the coachman's remark was quite correct, that it was a pity the day was so misty.}}
##* {{RQ:Charlotte Bronte Jane Eyre|volume=III|page=65|passage=Entering the gate and passing the shrubs, the '''silhouette''' of a house rose to view; black, low, and rather long: but the guiding light shone nowhere. All was obscurity.}}
##* {{RQ:Haggard Allan|chapter=Before the Statue|page=186|passage=Another second and I saw the black '''silhouette''' of the old Zulu raise its arm in mute salute, and Nyleptha was before us.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1950|month=January|author=Arthur F. Beckenham|title=With British Railways to the Far North|journal=w:The Railway Magazine|location=London|publisher=Tothill Press|page=5|issn=0033-8923|oclc=1256058197|passage=At Stirling, we obtained a wonderful '''silhouette''' view of the Wallace Monument, and the dark line of the Ochil Hills, and the castle stood out clearly against the afterglow of the sunset.}}
